digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56815-9
  rankdir="TB";
  "N1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently 2D semiconductors (TMDs) have potential to address scaling, heat, and memory walls in silicon ICs."];
  "N11" [label="(0,0,0) If the transfer technique enables the use of diverse materials and suppresses leakage via nanogaps, then it is a superior route for 2D dielectric integration."];
  "N13" [label="(11,0,0) Currently in-sensor computing expects heterostructure layers to be sensing or multifunctional to simplify structures."];
  "N15" [label="(0,0,0) If in-sensor computing requires structural simplification and sensing capabilities, then multifunctional dielectrics are a logical research direction."];
  "N18" [label="(13,0,0) Currently traditional gas-to-solid dielectrics are limited in material types and functions due to precursor restrictions."];
  "N2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently 2D semiconductors' characteristics like atomic thickness and vdW surfaces can boost FET and memory performance."];
  "N20" [label="(0,0,0) If traditional methods are limited in function and wet chemistry offers versatility, then wet chemistry should be applied to 2D dielectrics."];
  "N22" [label="(15,0,0) Currently merging solution-processed metal oxides with 2D devices is unexplored and offers possibilities if they are transferable."];
  "N23" [label="(0,0,0) If a specific solution-processed oxide can be prepared and transferred, it will realize the goal of a multifunctional 2D dielectric."];
  "N25" [label="(16,0,0) Currently we have prepared transferable amorphous copper calcium titanate (CCTO) via a wet-chemistry-derived method."];
  "N27" [label="(0,0,0) If CCTO is prepared via wet chemistry, is transferable, and has optical sensing properties, then it fulfills the requirement for a multifunctional 2D dielectric."];
  "N3" [label="(0,0,0) If a material has atomic-scale thickness and vdW surfaces that address IC challenges, then it is a promising candidate for next-generation electronics."];
  "N4" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given N1 and N2, therefore 2D semiconductors are promising for next-gen electronics."];
  "N6" [label="(5,0,0) Currently conventional ALD deposition on 2D surfaces results in poor nucleation and suboptimal interface quality."];
  "N7" [label="(0,0,0) If direct deposition methods fail to create high-quality interfaces on 2D materials, then alternative integration strategies are required."];
  "N8" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given N6 and N7, therefore alternative integration strategies are required for 2D dielectrics."];
  "N9" [label="(8,0,0) Currently the transfer technique allows separating fabrication and integration of dielectrics, loosening material and temperature restrictions."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper proposes, develops, and evaluates wet-chemistry-derived amorphous copper calcium titanate films that can be transferred as freestanding layers and used as multifunctional dielectrics in van der Waals-integrated TMD devices, aiming to combine high-quality transistor electrostatics, including 67 mV dec⁻¹ subthreshold swing and approximately 1 mV MV⁻¹ cm hysteresis, with optically writable and electrically erasable logic-in-sensor operation.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When 2D semiconductor devices require better dielectric integration and simplified multifunctional operation, a transferable wet-chemistry-derived oxide dielectric with optical and electrical functionality provides a coherent research strategy."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Because transfer techniques can separate dielectric fabrication from integration and transferred oxides can suppress gate leakage through nanogaps, transfer-based integration is a well-motivated route for integrating 2D dielectrics."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) The need to simplify in-sensor computing heterostructures motivates investigating multifunctional dielectrics as a research direction."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) The limitations of traditional gas-to-solid dielectric methods and the versatility of wet-chemistry metal oxides motivate applying wet-chemistry synthesis routes to 2D dielectrics."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Because solution-processed metal oxides may offer unexplored possibilities for 2D devices if transferable, it is reasonable to propose a prepared and transferable solution-processed oxide as a candidate multifunctional 2D dielectric."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Prepared transferable amorphous copper calcium titanate made by a wet-chemistry-derived method, together with optical writing and electrical erasing behavior for reconfigurable logic-in-sensor operation, supports CCTO as a candidate multifunctional dielectric for van der Waals-integrated 2D electronics."];
